      "hints": [
        "Integrate: $y = \\frac{(4x+1)^{3/2}}{6} + c$.",
        "Substitute $(2,5) \\implies 5 = \\frac{27}{6} + c \\implies c = \\frac{1}{2}$.",
        "Write $y = \\frac{(4x+1)^{3/2}}{6} + \\frac{1}{2}$."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Integrate $\\frac{dy}{dx} = (4x + 1)^{1/2}$ using the reverse chain rule:\n$$y = \\int (4x + 1)^{1/2}\\,dx = \\frac{(4x + 1)^{3/2}}{\\frac{3}{2} \\times 4} + c = \\frac{(4x + 1)^{3/2}}{6} + c$$",
        "Substitute the point $(2, 5)$ into the integrated expression to find $c$:\n$$5 = \\frac{(4(2) + 1)^{3/2}}{6} + c = \\frac{9^{3/2}}{6} + c = \\frac{27}{6} + c = \\frac{9}{2} + c \\implies c = 5 - \\frac{9}{2} = \\frac{1}{2}$$",
        "State the equation of the curve:\n$$y = \\frac{(4x + 1)^{3/2}}{6} + \\frac{1}{2}$$"
      ],
